  "response": "## What the finding actually points at\n\n`crime_00071` (`duplicate_component_shape`, confidence 0.70, medium) locates one `<div>` subtree in four places:\n\n| File | Lines |\n|---|---|\n| `dashboard/final-example/app/ui/invoices/create-form.tsx` | 142–150 |\n| `dashboard/final-example/app/ui/invoices/edit-form.tsx` | 152–160 |\n| `dashboard/starter-example/app/ui/invoices/create-form.tsx` | 101–109 |\n| `dashboard/starter-example/app/ui/invoices/edit-form.tsx` | 112–120 |\n\nReading all four, the subtree is the **form action bar**: `<div className=\"mt-6 flex justify-end gap-4\">` → a Cancel `<Link>` → a submit `<Button>`. I diffed them character by character. Exactly one token varies across the four copies:\n\n- `<Button type=\"submit\">` child text — `Create Invoice` vs `Edit Invoice`\n- Everything else is byte-identical: the wrapper classes, `href=\"/dashboard/invoices\"`, the Cancel link's ten utility classes, the label `Cancel`.\n\nThat's why this is worth extracting and also why it's low-risk: there is no behavioural divergence to reconcile, only one string to parameterise. `grep -rn \"justify-end gap-4\"` across the fixture returns those four lines and nothing else, so the extraction is complete — no fifth copy hiding elsewhere.\n\n## Proposed shared component\n\n`FormActions`, one per app at `app/ui/form-actions.tsx`.\n\n```tsx\nimport Link from 'next/link';\nimport { Button } from '@/app/ui/button';\n\nexport function FormActions({\n  submitLabel,\n  cancelHref = '/dashboard/invoices',\n  cancelLabel = 'Cancel',\n  submitDisabled = false,\n  children,\n}: {\n  submitLabel: string;              // required — the only real variation today\n  cancelHref?: string;              // default matches all 4 current call sites\n  cancelLabel?: string;\n  submitDisabled?: boolean;         // aria-disabled, for pending form state\n  children?: React.ReactNode;       // slot: extra actions, rendered left of Cancel\n}) {\n  return (\n    <div className=\"mt-6 flex justify-end gap-4\">\n      {children}\n      <Link\n        href={cancelHref}\n        className=\"flex h-10 items-center rounded-lg bg-gray-100 px-4 text-sm font-medium text-gray-600 transition-colors hover:bg-gray-200\"\n      >\n        {cancelLabel}\n      </Link>\n      <Button type=\"submit\" aria-disabled={submitDisabled}>\n        {submitLabel}\n      </Button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n**Public API**\n\n- **Props:** `submitLabel` (required, string); `cancelHref` (optional, defaults to `/dashboard/invoices`); `cancelLabel` (optional, defaults to `Cancel`); `submitDisabled` (optional boolean → `aria-disabled`, matching `Button`'s existing `aria-disabled:` styling).\n- **Slot:** `children`, rendered *before* Cancel so secondary actions (Delete, Save draft) land at the left of the right-aligned row. One slot is enough — nothing in the current four sites needs more.\n- **Deliberately not props:** the wrapper's `className` and the Cancel link's classes. Making layout configurable would re-open the drift this extraction closes. If a caller needs different spacing, that's a second component, not a prop.\n\nCall sites become one line each: `<FormActions submitLabel=\"Create Invoice\" />` / `<FormActions submitLabel=\"Edit Invoice\" />`.\n\n## Files that change\n\nSix files, two apps:\n\n1. **new** `dashboard/final-example/app/ui/form-actions.tsx`\n2. **new** `dashboard/starter-example/app/ui/form-actions.tsx`\n3. `dashboard/final-example/app/ui/invoices/create-form.tsx` — replace L142–150; drop the now-unused `Link` import (`Button` stays used only via `FormActions`, so drop that import too)\n4. `dashboard/final-example/app/ui/invoices/edit-form.tsx` — replace L152–160, same import cleanup\n5. `dashboard/starter-example/app/ui/invoices/create-form.tsx` — replace L101–109, same\n6. `dashboard/starter-example/app/ui/invoices/edit-form.tsx` — replace L112–120, same\n\n**Why two components, not one.** `final-example` and `starter-example` are separate Next.js apps with their own `package.json` and their own `@/` path alias — neither can import from the other. Collapsing all four sites into a single component would mean introducing a shared workspace package, which is a restructure well beyond what this finding justifies. Two components takes 4 copies → 2, and each app's create/edit pair (the pair that actually drifts together) is unified. If the two apps ever get a shared UI package, `FormActions` moves into it unchanged.\n\n## Two things this does not fix\n\n- **The bigger duplication is above the footer.** `create-form.tsx` and `edit-form.tsx` also share the customer `<select>`, the amount input, and the status `<fieldset>` — roughly 120 near-identical lines. The detector flagged only the footer because that's the exact structural match; the fieldset differs by `defaultChecked={invoice.status === 'pending'}` and by one class (`text-white-600` vs `text-gray-600` on the pending radio — likely a typo in `create-form`). That's a real second extraction (`<InvoiceStatusFieldset defaultStatus?>`), but it has genuine prop divergence, so it's a separate, larger change. Related findings `crime_00072`/`crime_00073` are the policy-check half of that same story.\n- **`design_token_escape` findings survive.** `crime_00074`/`00075`/`00078`/`00079` flag `h-[18px]`, `px-[14px]` etc., all of which live in the *upper* part of these files. The footer contains none of them, so those four findings stay put after this refactor.\n\nRe-running `crimes scan --format json` after the change should drop `crime_00071` — its fingerprint is `duplicate_component_shape::dashboard/final-example/app/ui/invoices/create-form.tsx::::e96b55b64336`, and with only one occurrence per shape remaining in each app, the shape no longer clears the multi-file threshold.",
